#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pool-run — work a pool of tickets to reviewed PRs, learning between them.

One ticket, one fresh headless session running `/resolve-ticket`. Between tickets, whenever enough
un-retroed run records have accumulated, one fresh session running `/skill-retro`. That split is not
an implementation convenience: `skill-retro`'s own design rests on a run being unable to grade its
own lessons, so the retro must not share a session with the run whose records it reads. And a fresh
session per ticket is what keeps run N+1 reading the skills as the retro left them.

What this driver decides, and what it deliberately does not:

  * It decides which ticket runs next, that the checkout is clean before it starts, that a session
    that stopped producing is dead, and what the RUN'S OUTCOME WAS — the last from GitHub and the
    gate state file, never from the model's closing prose. A session that says it opened a ready PR
    and did not is exactly the failure a driver exists to catch.
  * It decides nothing about the work. The prompt is `/resolve-ticket <url>` and nothing else: the
    skill owns its own autonomy contract, and a pipeline-level instruction bolted on here would be
    governance that no retro can see and no run record can cite. Anything this pipeline needs the
    runs to do belongs in the skill text, put there by a retro.

A claude.ai usage limit is not a failure and is not handled as one. The CLI's own wait-for-the-reset
is gated on an INTERACTIVE session, so it can never arm under the `claude -p` this driver runs: a
headless session simply ends the moment the window closes. So the driver reads the `rate_limit_event`
records the stream already carries, SUSPENDS the tickets in flight the way `--pause --now` does —
their transcripts intact, no attempt spent, worktrees kept — sleeps until the reset the CLI reported,
and re-enters them. See `wait_for_limit_reset` for the three cases where it hands back to a human
instead.

`--work` is carried past the same limit by a different mechanism, because neither of the other two
reaches it: the CLI's own wait belongs to the SESSION and can be gated off for an account, and the
driver's belongs to sessions it can suspend and re-enter, which a hand-launched one is not. So
`watch_hand_launched` notices the window reopening and tells the session to continue, over the local
peer socket the session itself publishes. It acts only on a run that is quiet AND that the ACCOUNT
refused a probe for; `ticket.limit_continue_work: false` turns it off.

Evidence the runs cannot produce about themselves is captured here: a run that dies leaves no record
of its own death, so this driver writes (or appends to) the record in `~/.claude/skill-lessons/`,
labelled as driver capture. It is capture, never a proposed rule — same contract the skills have.

Usage:
  pool-run                 work the pool until it is empty
  pool-run --once          work one ticket and stop
  pool-run --limit N       work at most N tickets
  pool-run --ticket 314    work one named ticket, label or no label
  pool-run --dry-run       preflight and print the queue; start nothing
  pool-run --status        print the ledger and stop
  pool-run --retro-now     run only the retro (ignores the record threshold)
  pool-run --no-retro      work tickets, never retro
  pool-run --init          create the pool label in each configured repo
  pool-run --pause         ask the running driver to stop at the next ticket boundary
  pool-run --pause --now   ... and suspend the sessions in flight, resumably, within seconds
  pool-run --resume        continue a paused pool from where it stopped
"""

